    "rich_text_intro": {
      "type": "rich-text",
      "blocks": {
        "text_intro": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>This is a Mediterranean platter built from the foods people actually eat in the world&rsquo;s blue zones &mdash; the handful of places where living past 100 is unremarkable. Okinawan sweet potatoes and chickpeas roast together on one sheet pan, Seatopia salmon bakes with nothing but olive oil, lemon and salt, and a honeyed dill tzatziki pulls the whole plate together.</p><p>It reads like a lot of components, and it is &mdash; but almost none of them need attention. The oven does the sweet potatoes and the salmon back to back on the same sheet pan. The eggs boil for seven minutes. The tzatziki and the cucumber salad are both stir-and-refrigerate. Fifteen minutes of hands-on prep, forty-five minutes of cooking, and it feeds three off one platter.</p><p>We built it around Seatopia <a href=\"/products/mt-cook-alpine-salmon-7oz-filet\" title=\"Alpine King Salmon from Mt. Cook\">Mt. Cook Alpine King Salmon</a> &mdash; 7 oz portions carrying roughly <strong>2,300 mg of marine omega-3s</strong> each, lab tested to the <a href=\"/pages/why-clean-seafood-matters\" title=\"Why Clean Seafood Matters\">Seatopia Standard&trade;</a>. Recipe by Seatopia partners <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/alexcbolivar\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Alex Bolivar</a> and <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/jasonpabst_\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Jason Pabst</a>.</p>",
            "text_size": "subtext-md",
            "text_max_size": 32,
            "text_font": "body",
            "secondary_color": false,
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        }
      },
