    "faq_cent": {
      "type": "faq",
      "blocks": {
        "q_okinawan": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "What is an Okinawan sweet potato, and can I substitute?",
            "text": "<p>Purple-fleshed, nuttier and drier than an orange sweet potato, and the purple is the point &mdash; that colour is anthocyanins. They are often sold as Japanese or purple sweet potatoes; a purple-skinned, white-fleshed one is a different variety but works fine. A regular orange sweet potato substitutes cleanly on texture, you just lose that particular pigment.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_jammy": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How do I get the yolks jammy rather than chalky?",
            "text": "<p><strong>Seven minutes</strong> at a genuine rolling boil, eggs lowered in rather than started cold, then straight into cold water for 15 minutes. The cold shock is what stops the cooking and makes them peel. Eight minutes gives you a firmer centre; ten and you are into hard-boiled and the choline argument gets weaker.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_onepan": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Do the sweet potatoes and salmon really share a pan?",
            "text": "<p>Yes, and it is the reason this works on a weeknight. Roast the vegetables at 425&deg;F for 30 minutes, tip them into a bowl, drop the oven to 350&deg;F and put the salmon on the same parchment. The residual oil and salt on the sheet is a feature. One pan, one wash-up.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_ahead": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How much of this can I build ahead?",
            "text": "<p>Most of it. Tzatziki keeps <strong>4 days</strong>, roasted sweet potato and chickpeas <strong>3 days</strong>, peeled eggs <strong>3 days</strong>, cooked salmon <strong>3 days</strong>. The cucumber-tomato salad is best the same day, and the avocado, sprouts and arugula should be cut and added right before serving. See the table above.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_salmon": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Does it have to be Mt. Cook salmon?",
            "text": "<p>No. We developed it with Mt. Cook Alpine King Salmon for the fat content, but any Seatopia fillet works and they all clear the same testing. Adjust the bake by a couple of minutes for thickness &mdash; a 6 oz Atlantic portion is done a touch sooner than a 7 oz Alpine King.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_defrost": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How do I defrost Seatopia salmon?",
            "text": "<p>Overnight in the refrigerator, wrapped, is best. In a hurry, put the vacuum-sealed portion in a bowl of cold water for 30&ndash;60 minutes. Pat it properly dry before it goes on the pan. Never thaw at room temperature or in the microwave.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "q_okinawan",
        "q_jammy",
        "q_onepan",
        "q_ahead",
        "q_salmon",
        "q_defrost"
      ],
